{
  "analysis": "Fresh shell at /app as root. No commands run yet. I need to locate the alpine.iso, confirm qemu is available, and understand the environment before launching the VM with a serial port redirected to a telnet server on port 6665.",
  "plan": "First explore: list files, check for qemu binaries, find the alpine.iso, and check what ISO-extract tools exist. This determines whether I can boot the ISO directly (e.g., alpine-virt already enables serial console) or need to extract the kernel/initramfs and pass console=ttyS0 explicitly.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "which qemu-system-x86_64 qemu-system-i386\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "qemu-system-x86_64 --version 2>&1 | head -n 1\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "find / -iname '*.iso' 2>/dev/null\n", "duration": 3.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "QEMU 5.2.0 is available. The target is /app/alpine.iso (~1GB, the standard Alpine ISO) plus a tiny alpine-disk.qcow2. To get a serial login prompt reachable via telnet, I need the guest kernel to output to ttyS0. The most reliable way is to extract the ISO's vmlinuz and initramfs and boot them directly with `console=ttyS0`, rather than relying on the ISO's bootloader. First I must determine what extraction tooling is available and inspect the ISO's boot config.",
  "plan": "1) Check which archive/iso tools exist (7z, isoinfo, bsdtar, xorriso) and whether loop mount is possible. 2) Mount or list the ISO to read the isolinux/grub config and confirm the kernel/initramfs filenames. This tells me the exact boot parameters needed.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "which 7z 7za isoinfo bsdtar xorriso mount losetup\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "ls -la /dev/loop* 2>/dev/null; echo done\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "mkdir -p /mnt/iso\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "mount -o loop,ro /app/alpine.iso /mnt/iso 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?\n", "duration": 1.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "No archive tools (7z/isoinfo/bsdtar/xorriso) and loop mount is blocked ('Operation not permitted'), so I cannot mount the ISO normally. I do have losetup but mount itself is denied. To boot with a serial console I likely need the ISO's kernel+initramfs and pass `console=ttyS0`, or confirm whether the ISO's bootloader already enables serial. I should check available interpreters and inspect the ISO's boot config by reading its contents.",
  "plan": "1) Check for python3 (I can write a small ISO9660 parser to extract vmlinuz/initramfs if needed). 2) Search the ISO bytes for the bootloader config (isolinux/grub 'append' lines and kernel filenames) to learn exact filenames and cmdline. This avoids needing mount. Based on results I'll decide whether to extract kernel/initrd or boot the ISO directly.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "which python3 python3.9 python3.10 strings\n", "duration": 0.2},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -a -o 'isolinux[^\"]*' /app/alpine.iso | head\n", "duration": 1.0},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -a -o 'vmlinuz[a-z0-9-]*' /app/alpine.iso | sort -u | head\n", "duration": 1.0},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -a -o 'initramfs[a-z0-9.-]*' /app/alpine.iso | sort -u | head\n", "duration": 1.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "No archive tools and loop mount is blocked, but python3 is available (I can parse ISO9660 if needed). The earlier grep for 'vmlinuz' returned nothing likely because ISO filenames are uppercase (e.g. VMLINUZ.VIR). The key question is whether the Alpine ISO's bootloader already emits console output to ttyS0. If the ISO's kernel cmdline includes `console=ttyS0`, I can simply boot the ISO directly with `-serial telnet:...` and avoid extraction entirely. Let me check the ISO's boot config for serial console support.",
  "plan": "Run case-insensitive greps on the ISO for ttyS0 and console= strings. If found, the simplest robust solution is to boot the ISO directly with QEMU attaching the first serial to a telnet server on 127.0.0.1:6665. If not present, I'll fall back to extracting the kernel/initramfs via a small python ISO9660 parser and boot with `console=ttyS0`.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "grep -ai 'ttyS0' /app/alpine.iso | head -5\n", "duration": 3.0},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -ai 'append' /app/alpine.iso | head -5\n", "duration": 3.0},
    {"keystrokes": "grep -ai 'serial ' /app/alpine.iso | head -5\n", "duration": 3.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Successfully extracted /tmp/vmlinuz-lts (11.8MB) and /tmp/initramfs-lts (20.5MB), and wrote /tmp/SYSLINUX.CFG. I need to read the cfg's APPEND line to replicate the boot cmdline exactly (so the live system starts correctly), then add `console=ttyS0` for serial output.",
  "plan": "Cat the SYSLINUX.CFG to see the kernel cmdline. Then I'll construct the QEMU launch. I'll preset the grub/syslinux SERIAL directive isn't there; I'll rely on `console=ttyS0` in the kernel cmdline. After that I'll write the mux and the launch script.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "cat /tmp/SYSLINUX.CFG\n", "duration": 0.2}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "QEMU (pid 462) and the mux (pid 463) are running. The mux connected to QEMU's serial port (6666) and is listening on 6665. Now I need to wait for Alpine to boot and confirm the serial console reaches a 'login:' prompt, which the mux will replay to any telnet client. Let me poll /tmp/serial.log.",
  "plan": "Poll /tmp/serial.log for the 'login:' prompt. Boot may take ~20-40s. I'll grep for 'login:' and also show the tail to confirm kernel/init progress. If the prompt isn't there yet, I'll wait and re-check.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "sleep 15; echo '--- serial.log size ---'; wc -c /tmp/serial.log; echo '--- tail ---'; tail -c 600 /tmp/serial.log; echo; echo '--- login check ---'; grep -a -o 'login:' /tmp/serial.log | tail -n 3\n", "duration": 16.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Alpine is booting well: it detected the QEMU DVD-ROM, mounted the boot media ('Mounting boot media: ok'), and is 24s in. No 'login:' yet, but boot isn't finished. Let me keep waiting and re-check the serial log.",
  "plan": "Wait more and poll /tmp/serial.log. Look for the 'login:' prompt specifically (getty on ttyS0). If Alpine's init is slow, wait another ~20-30s. I'll also watch for 'Welcome to Alpine' which precedes the login prompt.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "sleep 20; echo '--- size ---'; wc -c /tmp/serial.log; echo '--- tail ---'; tail -c 800 /tmp/serial.log; echo; echo '--- login check ---'; grep -a -o 'login:' /tmp/serial.log | tail -n 3\n", "duration": 21.0}
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The terminal display got garbled by interleaved output, but I can read that Alpine is now booting OpenRC and reached the banner ('########################'). Boot is nearly done. The serial.log is growing (28KB). I need a clean read to confirm the 'login:' prompt is present. Let me poll without extra echo noise.",
  "plan": "Run a minimal check: show size, tail, and grep for 'login:'. Wait a bit more if needed. Once 'login:' appears in /tmp/serial.log, the mux will replay it to a telnet client on 6665, satisfying the task.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "sleep 8; wc -c /tmp/serial.log; tail -c 400 /tmp/serial.log\n", "duration": 9.0}
  ]
}

